<p><strong>Setup and updates mean typing a few commands on a server. After that, everyone on the team works in a browser.</strong></p>
<p>
  Who does what: the owner rents the server and invites the team; a technical friend runs the commands below, sets up
  the web address and locks it down (see <a href="#security">Security notes</a>), and zips the
  <code>loginExtensionMulti</code> folder for the owner to send to each creator; each creator captures their session (signs in to OnlyFans
  once in Chrome so the relay can act on the account) on a computer; each chatter signs in from an invite link.
</p>
<p>
  If nobody on your team has done that before, ask someone who has, or read
  <a href="{{ '/openinflowwww-vs-infloww/#stay' | url }}">Who should stay on Infloww</a> first.
</p>

<h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>
<p>
  You need Docker Engine 25+ with Compose 2.20+. On Ubuntu 24.04, install it with
  <a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/">Docker's own Ubuntu instructions</a>; the distro's
  <code>docker.io</code> package may be older; check <code>docker version</code>. These are the steps from the README:
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Clone the repository (download the project's source folder) and <code>cd</code> into it.</li>
  <li>Create the external Docker network the app joins: <code>docker network create n8n_default</code>. (The network name comes from the maintainer's own Traefik setup; you don't need n8n.)</li>
  <li>Create the bind-mounted state files. They must be files, not directories.</li>
  <li>Write a <code>.env</code> with a share token and two session secrets.</li>
  <li>Start it.</li>
</ol>
<pre class="overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>{% include "quickstart-cmd.njk" %}</code></pre>
<p>Run this once. To update later, see <a href="#updating">Updating</a>.</p>

<h2 id="first-login">First login</h2>
<p>
  A server usually has no browser, and the app listens on 127.0.0.1 only. From your own computer, forward the port with
  <code>ssh -L 3001:127.0.0.1:3001 user@server</code>, then open <code>http://127.0.0.1:3001/?t=&lt;SHARE_TOKEN&gt;</code>
  in your browser. <code>grep SHARE_TOKEN .env</code> on the server prints the token; it is remembered after the first
  visit. The first screen is Sign in / Register at <code>/login</code>: register the owner account there first, then
  add each OnlyFans account by capturing its session, then invite chatters. Ignore the installer link on the login
  screen; it points at an older setup.
</p>

<h2 id="capture">Capture a session</h2>
{% include "capture-flow.njk" %}
<p>
  The creator's account will be seen from the server's IP (or the proxy you assign) from then on; assign a proxy before
  pasting if that matters.
</p>
<p>
  The desktop app's own capture button does not work against this relay today; use the Chrome extension. If a session
  stops working, capture it again the same way.
</p>

<h2 id="reaching-it">Reaching it from other machines</h2>
<p>
  Both a public domain and a quick tunnel make the dashboard public. Read the
  <a href="#security">security notes</a> first: use a private network or a VPN, or a second login in front of it; the
  tunnel is for trying it only.
</p>
<p>
  To give your chatters a normal web address, someone technical sets up a domain for the server once; the details are
  below.
</p>
<p>
  127.0.0.1 only works on the server itself, or through your <code>ssh -L</code> tunnel. Your laptop, your phone and your chatters use the server's URL. The
  relay and the app bind to 127.0.0.1 on the host, and the app also joins the external <code>n8n_default</code>
  network with Traefik labels; if you run Traefik on that network it will be published.
</p>
<p>
  One way is a domain behind a reverse proxy on the server: point Caddy or nginx at <code>127.0.0.1:3001</code>, or use
  the Traefik labels in <code>docker-compose.yml</code>, which expect your own Traefik on the <code>n8n_default</code>
  network with a <code>websecure</code> entrypoint and a cert resolver named <code>mytlschallenge</code>. Put your host
  (in place of <code>your-host.example.com</code>) and those names in a <code>docker-compose.override.yml</code>,
  which the repository already ignores, so <code>git pull</code> stays clean; don't edit the tracked file. The other is
  the <code>tunnel</code> profile: <code>docker compose --profile tunnel up -d</code> starts a Cloudflare quick tunnel,
  <code>docker compose logs openinflowwww-tunnel</code> shows its trycloudflare.com URL, and the URL changes on every
  restart.
</p>

<h2 id="security">Security notes</h2>
<p>
  In plain words: today anyone who can open your server's address can make an account on it, and a panel that
  restarts it needs no login. What the code does today:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Sign-up has no setting and needs no share token: anyone who reaches <code>/login</code> can register.</li>
  <li>Any registered account can list every user and every creator account on the server.</li>
  <li>The owner's and chatters' dashboard passwords are stored in plain text (<code>service/auth.py</code>). Creators'
    OnlyFans passwords never reach the server; the extension captures a session, not a password.</li>
  <li>The lanes panel (the relay's rate-limit page at <code>/lanes-panel</code>) can change the relay's limits and restart it with no login.</li>
  <li><code>ADMIN_PASSWORD</code> alone unlocks "view as another user", transfer and delete: any signed-in account that
    knows it can use them on any user.</li>
  <li>Logins have no rate limit.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Run it only on a private network or over a VPN (WireGuard or Tailscale) until these are fixed. Putting basic auth in
  front of the whole relay breaks fans' tracking links (<code>/t/</code>) and the desktop app, so if you must expose it,
  protect everything except <code>/t/</code> at your reverse proxy. All six are listed under
  <a href="{{ '/roadmap/#known-gaps' | url }}">Known gaps</a> on the roadmap.
</p>
<p>
  The passwords sit in <code>service/chatterly.db</code> and the proxy logins in <code>service/proxies.json</code>, so
  guard both like the sessions folder, and don't reuse a password from anywhere else.
</p>

<h2 id="team">Running a team</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Logins are a share token for the dashboard plus per-user logins: the owner at <code>/login</code>, each chatter at <code>/chatter-login</code>. Chatters sign in with their own login; the share token is for the owner (it does not stop anyone from registering; see <a href="#security">Security notes</a>).</li>
  <li>Invite each chatter from Employees &gt; Manage employees &gt; Chatters (<code>/employees</code>): under "Invite a new chatter", the "Mint invite URL" button makes a one-time <code>/chatter-login?token=...</code> link that expires after 24 hours, and they pick a username and password there.</li>
  <li>Owner pages such as Creators, Vault Pro and Tracking links are hidden from chatters.</li>
  <li>After signing in at <code>/chatter-login</code>, a chatter sees Dashboard, Messages Pro, Smart Messages, Scripts, Analytics and Settings for the creators they are linked to. In a chat they insert Scripts and attach media from the vault, limited to the folders the owner grants; a new chatter sees the whole vault until the owner picks folders.</li>
  <li>Fan notes live in the fan insights rail in Messages Pro (<code>/messages-pro</code>).</li>
  <li>There is no shift schedule yet: <code>/employees/shifts</code> is a NotBuiltYet stub.</li>
</ul>
<p>Moving from Infloww? See <a href="{{ '/switch-to-openinflowwww/' | url }}">Switch to Inflowwww</a>.</p>

<h2 id="optional-settings">Optional settings</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>ADMIN_PASSWORD</code>: optional. It is the only check on the admin actions (view as another user, transfer or delete a user, set another agency's AI keys), so anyone signed in who knows it can use them on any account. If you set it, use a long random value (<code>openssl rand -hex 32</code>) in <code>.env</code>, then run <code>docker compose up -d</code>. Without it those actions are refused with a 503.</li>
  <li>LLM keys (<code>DEEPSEEK_API_KEY</code>, <code>DEEPINFRA_API_KEY</code>, <code>ZAI_API_KEY</code>): the dashboard works without them.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="backups">Backups</h2>
<p>
  The state lives in files next to the compose file. The <code>service/</code> ones are bind-mounted into the relay;
  <code>.env</code> is read by Compose:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>service/chatterly.db</code>: the SQLite store.</li>
  <li><code>service/sessions/</code>: the captured sessions.</li>
  <li><code>service/proxies.json</code>: the proxy registry.</li>
  <li><code>service/secrets/</code>: the server keys and the admin password.</li>
  <li><code>.env</code>: the share token and the session secrets.</li>
  <li>The vault image folders (<code>service/vault_thumbs/</code>, <code>vault_posters/</code>, <code>vault_images/</code>): once OnlyFans' signed URLs expire, those images can't be downloaded again.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  The database runs in WAL mode inside the container, so do not just copy the file while the relay is running. Use
  SQLite's backup API from inside the container. From the install folder:
</p>
<pre class="overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>mkdir -p backups
docker exec openinflowwww-relay python -c "import sqlite3; d = sqlite3.connect('/tmp/chatterly-backup.db'); sqlite3.connect('/app/service/chatterly.db').backup(d); d.close()"
docker cp openinflowwww-relay:/tmp/chatterly-backup.db backups/chatterly-$(date +%F).db
sudo tar czf backups/state-$(date +%F).tgz service/sessions service/proxies.json service/secrets service/vault_thumbs service/vault_posters service/vault_images .env</code></pre>
<p>
  The relay runs as root and keeps the secrets file readable by root only, which is why <code>tar</code> needs
  <code>sudo</code>. <code>chatterly.db</code> and <code>proxies.json</code> hold passwords in plain text, so encrypt
  each archive before it leaves the server (with <code>age</code> or <code>gpg</code>) and keep the key somewhere
  other than the backup.
</p>
<p>
  To test a backup, restore it to a scratch copy. Stock Debian and Ubuntu images don't ship the <code>sqlite3</code>
  command, so install it once with <code>sudo apt install sqlite3</code>.
</p>
<pre class="overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>mkdir -p restore-test
cp backups/chatterly-2026-10-12.db restore-test/chatterly.db
sqlite3 restore-test/chatterly.db "PRAGMA integrity_check;"   # prints: ok</code></pre>
<p>A real restore means stopping the stack, putting the files back, and starting it again:</p>
<pre class="overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>docker compose down
cp backups/chatterly-2026-10-12.db service/chatterly.db
sudo tar xzf backups/state-2026-10-12.tgz
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
<p>
  <code>docker compose down</code> lets SQLite checkpoint and close cleanly. The compose file asks you to avoid
  <code>docker kill</code> for the same reason.
</p>

<h2 id="updating">Updating</h2>
<p>
  Back up first: <code>service/chatterly.db</code>, <code>service/sessions/</code>, <code>service/proxies.json</code>,
  <code>service/secrets/</code>, <code>.env</code> and the vault folders (<code>service/vault_thumbs/</code>,
  <code>service/vault_posters/</code>, <code>service/vault_images/</code>). Copy the database with the
  <a href="#backups">backup command above</a> (sqlite3's backup API), not <code>cp</code>, while it runs. Then, from the install folder:
</p>
<pre class="overflow-x-auto whitespace-pre"><code>git pull &amp;&amp; docker compose up -d --build</code></pre>
<p>
  Never re-paste the Quick start on a running install. From inside the install folder it clones a second copy into
  <code>openinflowwww/openinflowwww</code> and restarts the containers on that copy's empty database and new
  <code>.env</code>, so the dashboard comes back empty and everyone is signed out. Your data is still in the original
  folder: <code>cd</code> back to it and run <code>docker compose up -d</code>.
</p>

